# Line endings.
#
# Everything in this repository that executes - the shell scripts, the
# Python, the SDF world files - runs on Linux: WSL, PX4, Gazebo. But the
# repository is often cloned on Windows, and Git for Windows ships with
# core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so it rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout.
#
# That breaks a shell script silently. bash reads the first line as
# "set -e\r" and reports:
#
#     set: - : invalid option
#     syntax error: unexpected end of file
#
# Nothing looks wrong: git status is clean, the file opens fine in an
# editor, and the blob in the object database is correct LF. Only the
# working copy is broken, and the error message points nowhere near the
# cause. Measured on a Windows clone of this repository: sim/install.sh
# came out with 26 CR bytes and would not run.
#
# Forcing LF on checkout removes the whole class of problem.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched by end-of-line conversion. The QR
# textures in particular: a single flipped byte changes what the code
# decodes to, and no test would catch it.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
